--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::constant` refuses a constant whose declared type carries `secret` at every site, the declaring class's own included, because the `mixed` it answers with carries no qualifier — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`: a `secret` value is refused at the sinks that would
// disclose it, and the qualifier is what a sink checks. This member answers
// `mixed`, which carries none — so a `secret string` handed through it would be
// an ordinary string at the next sink and the refusal would never be made.
// Refusing here is the direction that fails closed.
class Vault {
    public const secret string TOKEN = "sk-live";
    public const string LABEL = "vault";

    public function fromInside(): string {
        var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject($this);
        try {
            return $described->constant("TOKEN") as string;
        } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
            return "refused inside";
        }
    }
}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Vault());

// The roster still names it, `isPublic` still says code outside may name it and
// `hasValue` still says the declaration folded: the refusal is about handing the
// value *out*, and it is neither a visibility answer nor a missing value.
// `rule:core-classes/reflect` asks that a refusal stay distinguishable from a
// misspelling, and this is that rule with one more reason to refuse.
foreach ($described->constants() as Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo $constant) {
    echo $constant->name(), " ", $constant->isPublic() ? "public" : "closed", " ",
         $constant->hasValue() ? "value" : "none", "\n";
}

// The unqualified constant beside it reads normally, so what is being pinned is
// the qualifier and not the member.
echo $described->constant("LABEL") as string, "\n";

// Outside the class, where an ordinary read would succeed — the constant is
// `public`, so visibility is not what refuses here.
try {
    $described->constant("TOKEN");
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "refused outside\n";
}

// And inside it, which is what makes this a different rule from visibility: the
// value is still reachable by naming `Vault::TOKEN` in source, where the
// qualifier survives the read.
echo (new Vault())->fromInside(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
TOKEN public value
LABEL public value
vault
refused outside
refused inside
